Z||000000||Shaun41178||12-05-2001||06:48 PM||||ok I would like to know the differences from a 3.4 dohc block to a aluminum v6 block.  I do know the lifter valley on the dohc block is closed off.  On my aluminum one though however it is not as it was meant for a pushrod setup.<P>Well basically I am wondering if it would be possible to stuff the DOHC heads and everything onto this aluminum block.  I know it is such a dumb question and I should probably know better but I ahve never seen a dohc block up close and personal to see for myself.  So I have no basis of compare. <P>Basically the aluminum block is setup just like the iron block if that helps at all<P>i know a few people on here know a lot about the dohc block so I am hoping you can help me out.<P>------------------<BR>Sold Nitrous 85 GT.<BR>Looking for another.<BR>FOR SALE: Aluminum v6 block.<BR>Shaun41178@hotmail.com<BR>PORN FOREVER!||||reg||1
Z||000001||88-DOHC||12-05-2001||07:01 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Shaun41178:<BR><B>ok I would like to know the differences from a 3.4 dohc block to a aluminum v6 block.  I do know the lifter valley on the dohc block is closed off.  On my aluminum one though however it is not as it was meant for a pushrod setup.<P>Well basically I am wondering if it would be possible to stuff the DOHC heads and everything onto this aluminum block.  I know it is such a dumb question and I should probably know better but I ahve never seen a dohc block up close and personal to see for myself.  So I have no basis of compare. <P>Basically the aluminum block is setup just like the iron block if that helps at all<P>i know a few people on here know a lot about the dohc block so I am hoping you can help me out.<P></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>From camparisons of the 2.8 Fiero motor and 3.4 DOHC, the blocks appear to be exactly the same visually. Whether the bore size is the same, probably not.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000002||DRH||12-05-2001||07:11 PM||||The DOHC is definitely a very similar block, so it's not a silly question. It still has a shaft running where the OHV cam would be which drives the oil pump. It even has the distributor hold down clamp holding a plug in.<P>The problem might be in the oiling. There's a wierd looking aluminum piece bolted into the bottom of the V over the "cam". It looks like it has oil passages through it. I have a DOHC in my garage partially disassembled. I'll pull that piece off tonight and see what's underneath.||||reg||1
Z||000003||00lE||12-05-2001||07:41 PM||||Although the DOHC block has provisions for a distrubutor, the DOHC uses a distributorless iginition system.  I do not believe that the V6 aluminum bowtie block has a hole machined for the crankshaft position sensor, so engine control would be a big problem unless you could mount a distributor somewhere.  The lack of a crankshaft position sensor hole would probably also be a big issue with using the GEN III heads and intake on the bowtie block.<p>[This message has been edited by 00lE (edited 12-05-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000004||DRH||12-05-2001||08:12 PM||||The center valley is a <B>completely</B> different casting from the OHV engines. It's not just leaving out the lifter bores, it has oil passages cast in to take oil to the heads. I don't have one to look at, but I'm pretty sure the OHV block would not have these since they use the pushrods for oil distribution.<P>Too bad... that would have been one lightweight, high reving, screamer.<P>||||reg||1
Z||000005||Standard||12-05-2001||08:32 PM||||&lt;dream&gt;hmm... bore out the al. block as much as you can, de-stroke it, DOHC heads... solid cam followers, Ti valve springs, custom cams... Ti connecting rods... 10,000 rpm anyone?  uggghghghghg&lt;/dream&gt;  I think I have to go change boxers now..  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"><P>------------------<BR>      <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/sig_pic_full.jpg">          <BR><FONT COLOR="red">1988 Quad 4 Coupe 5 speed</FONT><BR><FONT SIZE="1"><FONT COLOR="white">1986 GT 5 speed, 1984 SC Auto</FONT><BR><FONT COLOR="blue">AOL: LastMinuteBastrd  AIM: SavedbyJebus</FONT></FONT>||||reg||1
Z||000006||Shaun41178||12-05-2001||10:56 PM||||The bore size of the aluminum block can be made as big as a 3.4 block.<P>Yes I also knew about it having like a speudo camshaft as it uses a similar setup to drive the oil pump.<P>Yes the aluminum block does have a hole drilled for a crank angle sensor which means it can use a distributorless ignition system no problem.  I give credit for GM as they were thinking ahead here.<P>DRH  Yes I had heard of the valley section being different.  However without ever seeing one up close I have no idea how different it is.  But yes I do believe you are right about the oil passages being different in the DOHC block and the aluminum block.  I have seen diagrams a long time ago but dont' remember specifics but was thinking something could be adapted to get the oil up to the cam lobes in the head.<P>Anyone have pics they can post?<P>Joe Torma if you see this post can you post the pics that I sent you?  If not that is no big deal.  I dont' have a scanner otherwise I would be able to.  hopefully if Joe is able to get pics up of my aluminum block that I sent him you can all see and make a guess based on that.<P>I was doubting it would be able to work but I had to ask.  Yes it would be nice to have a 2.9 DOHC that goes to 8k rpm.<P>hopefully the questions can be answered and have this put to rest once and for all.  Thanks for anymore info anyone can provide.<P>------------------<BR>Sold Nitrous 85 GT.<BR>Looking for another.<BR>FOR SALE: Aluminum v6 block.<BR>Shaun41178@hotmail.com<BR>PORN FOREVER!||||reg||1
Z||000007||DRH||12-06-2001||12:01 AM||||I wish I had a digital camera. I just went and looked at it again. It doesn't really have a valley, it's level with the distributor plug mounting surface with a flat raised area down the middle that an aluminum oil distribution and breather cover mounts too. Even if you can get oil to the heads, you'd still need to plug the lifter oil holes and some how seal off the whole area (the area under the intake is open to the outside).<P>If anybody ever did it, I would be trully amazed!!!||||reg||1
